WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.742
Last time, one spring, where Newton and energy were equally easy.

00:00:05.742 --> 00:00:09.187
With more springs, which one is easier?

00:00:09.187 --> 00:00:12.720
Let's find out, on two springs in a row.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:21.220
A wall, spring one, a junction we'll call node one, spring two, and the end, node two.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:29.707
There's a load on each node, F one and F two, and each node moves right: q one and q two.

00:00:29.707 --> 00:00:31.900
Those are our unknowns.

00:00:32.233 --> 00:00:37.884
Before anything else we need one fact: how much does each spring stretch?

00:00:37.884 --> 00:00:39.355
Spring one is easy.

00:00:39.355 --> 00:00:45.393
Its left end is on the wall, its right end moves q one, so it stretches q one.

00:00:45.727 --> 00:00:47.876
Spring two is the one to watch.

00:00:47.876 --> 00:00:49.401
Both of its ends move.

00:00:49.401 --> 00:00:53.283
Its left end moves q one, and its right end moves q two.

00:00:53.283 --> 00:00:56.887
So its stretch is the difference: q two minus q one.

00:00:57.227 --> 00:01:02.909
If both ends moved the same amount, the spring would just slide along without stretching at all.

00:01:02.909 --> 00:01:04.507
Only the difference counts.

00:01:06.067 --> 00:01:10.224
First, Newton's way, so we have something to compare with.

00:01:10.224 --> 00:01:13.307
Cut out node one and list the forces on it.

00:01:13.607 --> 00:01:17.674
Spring one pulls it back, to the left, with k one q one.

00:01:17.674 --> 00:01:24.283
Spring two pulls it forward, to the right, with k two times its stretch, q two minus q one.

00:01:24.283 --> 00:01:26.607
And the load F one pushes right.

00:01:28.207 --> 00:01:36.007
Balance: minus k one q one, plus k two times q two minus q one, plus F one, equals zero.

00:01:36.307 --> 00:01:47.417
Multiply out and move F one across: minus k one q one, plus k two q two, minus k two q one, equals minus F one.

00:01:47.417 --> 00:01:58.027
Flip every sign and group the q one terms: k one plus k two, times q one, minus k two q two, equals F one.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:07.613
Node two: spring two pulls it back with k two times q two minus q one, and F two pulls forward.

00:02:07.613 --> 00:02:13.846
So minus k two times q two minus q one, plus F two, equals zero.

00:02:13.846 --> 00:02:20.080
Multiply out: minus k two q one, plus k two q two, equals F two.

00:02:20.414 --> 00:02:22.727
Two equations, two unknowns.

00:02:22.727 --> 00:02:24.627
Keep these on the side.

00:02:24.627 --> 00:02:29.254
Now let's get the very same equations from energy alone.

00:02:30.666 --> 00:02:32.451
Strain energy first.

00:02:32.451 --> 00:02:38.699
Each spring stores one half, its stiffness, times its stretch squared.

00:02:38.699 --> 00:02:44.946
Then we add them up, because energy is just a number, and numbers add.

00:02:45.246 --> 00:02:50.655
Spring one stores one half k one q one squared.

00:02:50.655 --> 00:02:58.366
Spring two stores one half k two, times q two minus q one, squared.

00:02:58.699 --> 00:03:05.335
Pay close attention to that second term, because this is where most mistakes happen.

00:03:05.335 --> 00:03:11.339
It's the stretch, q two minus q one, that gets squared, not q two by itself.

00:03:11.639 --> 00:03:21.359
The work potential is each load times the displacement of its own node: F one q one plus F two q two.

00:03:21.739 --> 00:03:23.921
And Pi is U minus W.

00:03:23.921 --> 00:03:27.086
One function of two unknowns.

00:03:27.086 --> 00:03:36.579
The stationary principle says: at the real answer, Pi is stationary in every direction.

00:03:37.633 --> 00:03:47.513
With two unknowns, stationary means: the partial derivative with respect to q one is zero, and the partial derivative with respect to q two is zero.

00:03:47.813 --> 00:03:49.241
Take q one first.

00:03:49.241 --> 00:03:53.190
One half k one q one squared gives k one q one.

00:03:53.190 --> 00:04:04.533
For the second spring, use the chain rule: two times one half is one, times k two times q two minus q one, times the inside derivative.

00:04:04.828 --> 00:04:11.266
And the inside derivative, the derivative of q two minus q one with respect to q one, is minus one.

00:04:11.266 --> 00:04:15.948
That minus sign is why our equations have minus signs between neighbors.

00:04:16.246 --> 00:04:23.024
Then the load: F one q one gives F one, with a minus sign.

00:04:23.024 --> 00:04:34.126
Set it all to zero: k one q one, minus k two q two, plus k two q one, minus F one, equals zero.

00:04:34.426 --> 00:04:46.676
Group the q one terms and move F one across: k one plus k two, times q one, minus k two q two, equals F one.

00:04:46.676 --> 00:04:50.306
Exactly Newton's first equation.

00:04:50.606 --> 00:04:51.594
Now q two.

00:04:51.594 --> 00:05:02.757
Only two terms contain it: the second spring gives k two times q two minus q one, and the load gives minus F two.

00:05:02.757 --> 00:05:10.464
Set to zero and multiply out: minus k two q one plus k two q two equals F two.

00:05:10.464 --> 00:05:12.933
Newton's second equation.

00:05:12.933 --> 00:05:15.206
We never drew an arrow.

00:05:16.833 --> 00:05:21.082
Stack the two equations into one matrix equation.

00:05:21.082 --> 00:05:25.677
Row one is node one's balance; row two is node two's.

00:05:25.677 --> 00:05:29.753
Each equation's coefficients drop into its row.

00:05:30.086 --> 00:05:33.675
The matrix is called the stiffness matrix.

00:05:33.675 --> 00:05:39.486
Like K for one spring, it multiplies displacements and gives forces.

00:05:39.786 --> 00:05:41.096
Now numbers.

00:05:41.096 --> 00:05:47.099
k one is forty newtons per millimeter, k two is twenty.

00:05:47.099 --> 00:05:51.465
F one is forty newtons, F two is twenty.

00:05:51.465 --> 00:05:57.906
So the matrix is sixty, minus twenty, minus twenty, twenty.

00:05:58.206 --> 00:06:03.966
Your turn: pause and do checkpoint two point one in your guide, and solve for q one and q two.

00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:15.406
Add the two equations: the q two terms cancel, leaving forty q one equals sixty, so q one is one point five millimeters.

00:06:15.406 --> 00:06:23.646
Then the second equation: twenty times q two minus one point five equals twenty, so q two is two point five millimeters.

00:06:23.917 --> 00:06:25.654
Quick check with Newton.

00:06:25.654 --> 00:06:35.718
Cut through spring one: everything to its right carries forty plus twenty, sixty newtons, so it stretches sixty over forty, one point five.

00:06:35.718 --> 00:06:39.990
Spring two carries only F two, twenty, so it stretches one.

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:43.103
Two point five minus one point five is one.

00:06:43.103 --> 00:06:44.117
It all agrees.

00:06:45.765 --> 00:06:47.256
So which way was easier?

00:06:47.256 --> 00:06:49.243
For two springs, about the same.

00:06:49.243 --> 00:06:51.913
But picture a node with five springs on it.

00:06:51.913 --> 00:06:56.570
Newton needs five arrows, each with the right direction and the right sign.

00:06:56.570 --> 00:06:58.805
Energy needs one more term in a sum.

00:06:59.105 --> 00:07:05.585
That's the first reason: energy is a scalar, a single number, with no directions to keep track of.

00:07:06.518 --> 00:07:08.661
And the picture still works.

00:07:08.661 --> 00:07:12.104
With two unknowns, Pi is a bowl over a floor.

00:07:12.104 --> 00:07:15.854
One floor direction is q one, the other is q two.

00:07:15.854 --> 00:07:24.118
The answer is the lowest point of the bowl: one point five and two point five, where Pi is minus fifty five.

00:07:25.018 --> 00:07:27.005
Second reason: it scales.

00:07:27.005 --> 00:07:34.713
For a hundred springs, you write one sum for U, one sum for W, and take derivatives mechanically.

00:07:34.713 --> 00:07:39.799
A computer can do that without ever drawing a free-body diagram.

00:07:39.799 --> 00:07:42.898
That's exactly what finite elements do.

00:07:44.065 --> 00:07:48.957
Here's an example from the course slides, built one spring at a time.

00:07:48.957 --> 00:07:50.800
Node one sits on the wall.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:53.635
Spring k one joins node one to node two.

00:07:53.635 --> 00:07:56.825
Spring k three joins node three to node four.

00:07:57.125 --> 00:08:03.792
Between nodes two and three there are two springs, both k two, side by side.

00:08:03.792 --> 00:08:14.318
Both share the same two end points, so they stretch by the same amount, and together they store twice the energy of one.

00:08:14.318 --> 00:08:17.125
That's why you'll see two k two.

00:08:17.425 --> 00:08:34.105
So U is one half k one, q two minus q one, squared, plus two times one half k two, q three minus q two, squared, plus one half k three, q four minus q three, squared.

00:08:34.405 --> 00:08:40.820
The loads are F two and F four, so W is F two q two plus F four q four.

00:08:40.820 --> 00:08:45.337
Take the partial derivative with respect to q two.

00:08:45.337 --> 00:08:50.125
It appears in two terms: k one's, and the k two pair.

00:08:50.425 --> 00:08:58.955
That gives k one times q two minus q one, minus two k two times q three minus q two, minus F two.

00:08:58.955 --> 00:09:10.385
Set it to zero and multiply out: minus k one q one, plus k one plus two k two, times q two, minus two k two q three, equals F two.

00:09:10.685 --> 00:09:14.553
Do that for every node, and you get this four by four matrix.

00:09:14.553 --> 00:09:16.645
Row two is the one we just built.

00:09:17.765 --> 00:09:22.485
Look at that matrix one spring at a time, and a pattern jumps out.

00:09:22.785 --> 00:09:34.385
A spring of stiffness k between nodes i and j puts plus k on the diagonal at i, plus k on the diagonal at j, and minus k in the two spots that connect them.

00:09:34.718 --> 00:09:44.638
This is the key idea for everything that follows: each spring brings its own little two by two block, and where blocks share a node, the entries simply add.

00:09:44.972 --> 00:09:50.212
Use the pattern: pause and do checkpoint two point two in your guide, and write row three.

00:09:53.445 --> 00:09:58.534
Row three: minus two k two, then two k two plus k three, then minus k three.

00:09:58.534 --> 00:10:01.078
And notice: every row adds up to zero.

00:10:01.078 --> 00:10:06.703
Shift every node by the same amount, and no spring stretches, so no force is needed.

00:10:06.703 --> 00:10:08.845
That's what a zero row sum says.

00:10:09.178 --> 00:10:15.077
But node one is on the wall: q one is zero, and the wall supplies whatever force it needs.

00:10:15.077 --> 00:10:19.075
So its row and column don't belong in the equations we solve.

00:10:19.075 --> 00:10:23.925
Cross them out, and three equations remain, for q two, q three and q four.

00:10:23.925 --> 00:10:28.578
Our two-spring matrix was like that too: its wall node was never in it.

00:10:28.912 --> 00:10:39.808
So, stepping back: write U and W, take one partial derivative per free node, and the stiffness matrix builds itself from little blocks.

00:10:39.808 --> 00:10:42.552
Next, your first homework problem.
